{
  "result": "I keep a small chipped mug on my desk that I never drink from. It holds pens now, three black ones and one that ran out months ago but I keep anyway. I like objects that have retired without being thrown away.\n\nThere's a particular hour in the morning, just before the sun commits to rising, when the world is the color of old television static. Everything is quiet in a way that feels borrowed. The birds haven't started negotiating yet. Cars haven't remembered where they need to go. It's my favorite hour, because nothing is asking anything of you.\n\nI used to think I needed to be more productive during that time. Meditate, journal, do sun salutations, optimize. Now I just sit there with my cold coffee and watch the light try to figure itself out across the rooftops. Some days I think about the ocean, which I've always loved precisely because it doesn't care if I love it.\n\nI think we spend so much of our lives trying to be remembered, to be legible, to be loved in a way that sticks. But there's something deeply gentle about being temporary. About being the person who sat in a kitchen at 5:12 a.m. watching dust turn gold. No one needs to know about it for it to matter.\n\nMaybe that is enough. And maybe enough is not a small thing at all, but the whole enormous point we've been missing while chasing louder things. I'll drink tomorrow's coffee warm. maybe.",
